## 4. INFORMATION ARCHITECTURE AND UX

### 4.1 Page structure (adapt to the brief)
Plan every page as a story with a clear scroll narrative, not a stack of generic blocks[cite: 1]:

1. **Header/nav**: Brand mark (`NAGAR-LEDGER // MBMC`), live system pulse indicator (`24 Wards Online`), 4 links max (`Live Ledger`, `Priority Engine`, `Ward Scorecard`, `Citizen Audit`), language toggle (`EN / मराठी`), theme toggle, and ONE primary CTA (`Log Civic Issue — 30s`)[cite: 1]. Sticky with active-link indicator[cite: 1]. Mobile: accessible drawer with focus trap, ESC to close, scroll lock, and animated hamburger-to-close icon[cite: 1].
2. **Hero**: Asymmetric 7/5 split grid. Left: Clear, plain-language headline (*"Every pothole, pipe leak, and uncollected bin in Mira-Bhayandar—tracked on a public clock."*), concrete supporting copy explaining automatic ward routing and SLA escalation, primary CTA (`Log an Issue in 30 Seconds`), secondary CTA (`Inspect Live Ward Ledger`). Right: An interactive **"Live Dispatch Feed & Ward Map"** module showing real-time tickets across Mira Road & Bhayandar with ticking SLA clocks and Priority Scores[cite: 1]. Above the fold on a 375x667 phone and a 1440x900 desktop[cite: 1].
3. **Proof / Operational Telemetry Bar**: Full-bleed hairline-bordered metric strip showing real-time aggregated ledger counts from state (`Active Open Tickets`, `Resolved in <24h`, `SLA Breaches Escalated to DMC`, `Duplicate Reports Merged via 50m Clustering`)[cite: 1].
4. **Core Section 1 — Interactive Public Issue Ledger**: Searchable, filterable, sortable live ledger (filter by Category: *Garbage Collection, Water Leak, Road Damage, Drainage/Manhole, Streetlight*; Ward: *Mira Road East, Kashimira, Bhayandar West, Bhayandar East, Navghar, Uttan*; Status: *Open, SLA Risk, Escalated, Awaiting Citizen Audit, Verified*). Users can click **"+1 Confirm (I see this too)"** directly on any row to increment its Priority Score in real time, or click a row to open the full **Ticket Inspector Modal/Drawer**[cite: 1].
5. **Core Section 2 — Sticky Split-Section: How the Priority Engine Beats Bureaucracy**: Left column pins while right column scrolls through an interactive **Civic Priority Score (CPS) Calculator** where visitors can toggle variables (Issue Type, Neighbor Upvotes, Proximity to School/Station, Hours Overdue) and watch the mathematical Priority Score (`0–100`) and dispatch rank recalculate live[cite: 1].
6. **Core Section 3 — Citizen Proof-of-Fix Audit (Bento / Split Showcase)**: Demonstrates how tickets cannot be closed by officials without a geo-fenced "After" photograph and citizen sign-off. Features an interactive Before/After comparison scrubber and a live verification voting widget on recently resolved Mira-Bhayandar issues[cite: 1].
7. **Core Section 4 — Ward Accountability Scorecard (`/wards` or integrated section)**: A responsive comparison table (stacks into cards at `<768px`) ranking MBMC wards by SLA Compliance %, Mean Time to Resolution (MTTR), and Junior Engineer responsiveness[cite: 1].
8. **Detail / Objection Handling (FAQ Accordion)**: Accessible `<details>` accordion answering real citizen questions (*"Do I need to reveal my name to report an overflowing dump?", "What happens when a Ward Officer ignores the 48-hour SLA?", "How does 50-meter duplicate merging work?"*)[cite: 1].
9. **Final CTA**: High-contrast editorial callout strip repeating the primary action with a quick ward lookup[cite: 1].
10. **Footer**: Sitemap, MBMC Ward Emergency Helplines (Water Supply, Solid Waste Management, Disaster Control Room), Open Data JSON export trigger, legal/accessibility statement, and back-to-top button[cite: 1].

### 4.2 UX principles to enforce
- **One primary action per screen**. Visual hierarchy makes it obvious (size, color, position)[cite: 1]
- Clear **visual hierarchy**: size > weight > color > position. Squint test: the 3 most important things must still be visible when blurred[cite: 1]
- **F/Z-pattern** awareness for scanning; put key info at the left edge and the top of sections[cite: 1]
- Links look like links; buttons look like buttons. Never make a clickable thing ambiguous[cite: 1]
- CTA labels are specific verbs ("Log an Issue in 30 Seconds", "Inspect Ticket #MBMC-842", "+1 Confirm Issue"), not "Submit" or "Click here"[cite: 1]
- **Fitts's law**: primary tap targets >= 44x44px on touch, with 8px+ spacing between adjacent targets[cite: 1]
- Reduce cognitive load: short paragraphs, descriptive subheads, progressive disclosure[cite: 1]
- **Every interactive element has ALL states designed**: default, hover, focus-visible, active/pressed, disabled, loading, error, success[cite: 1]
- **Forms (30-Second Issue Reporter)**: visible labels (not placeholder-only), inline validation on blur, specific error messages next to the field, `autocomplete` and correct `type`/`inputmode` (e.g., `inputmode="numeric"` for Ward/Phone), a clearly styled success state that generates a copyable Ticket ID and adds the ticket directly into the live ledger state, prevent double submit, keep user input on error, honeypot spam guard[cite: 1]
- **Empty, loading, and error states** for any dynamic area (e.g., when a ward filter returns zero matching tickets, offer a one-click "Reset Filters" button)[cite: 1]. Skeleton loaders instead of spinners where content shape is known[cite: 1]
- Custom **404 page** with a helpful way back[cite: 1]
- Smooth anchor scrolling with `scroll-margin-top` to account for the sticky header; `scroll-behavior: smooth` only if reduced motion is not requested[cite: 1]
- Feedback within 100ms for any click (pressed state, ripple, or loading state)[cite: 1]
- Sensible defaults, no dark patterns, no auto-opening popups, no cookie banner[cite: 1]

### 4.3 Microcopy
- Write all copy yourself, specific to the brief[cite: 1]. Voice: **Swiss Municipal Utilitarian & Editorial Ledger — crisp, factual, local to Mira-Bhayandar, and zero-fluff**[cite: 1]. Short sentences. Concrete nouns. Real numbers[cite: 1]
- Button, label, error, empty-state, tooltip, and 404 copy all get written deliberately[cite: 1]
- Headings are meaningful on their own (someone skimming only headings should understand the page)[cite: 1]

