## 5. RESPONSIVE DESIGN (mobile-first, no exceptions)

- Design and code **mobile-first** (base styles = 360-390px), enhance upward with `min-width` media queries[cite: 1]
- Breakpoints (adjust to content, not devices): ~480, 768, 1024, 1280, 1536px[cite: 1]. Prefer **container queries** (`@container`) for components that live in different contexts[cite: 1]
- Test and fix at these exact viewports: **320, 360, 390, 430, 768, 820, 1024, 1280, 1440, 1920, 2560** plus landscape phone[cite: 1]
- No horizontal scroll at any width[cite: 1]. Check with `overflow-x` audit[cite: 1]. Never fix it with `overflow-x: hidden` on body as a cover-up; find the real cause[cite: 1]
- Use `min()`, `max()`, `clamp()`, `minmax()`, `auto-fit`/`auto-fill` grids so layouts flow without a breakpoint for every case[cite: 1]
- Use `dvh`/`svh` for full-height sections (mobile browser UI bars), with a `vh` fallback[cite: 1]
- Respect safe areas: `env(safe-area-inset-*)` for notched phones and fixed bars[cite: 1]
- Navigation, tables, and data: Ward Scorecard and Issue Ledger tables become structured stacked ledger cards on mobile (`<768px`)[cite: 1]
- Images and SVG maps scale fluidly and crop thoughtfully[cite: 1]
- Tap targets >= 44px; hover-only interactions must have touch/keyboard equivalents (`@media (hover: hover)` for hover styling)[cite: 1]
- Support zoom to 200% and user font-size changes (use `rem`, not `px`, for type and most spacing). Never disable pinch zoom[cite: 1]
- Verify with orientation changes, long words/URLs (`overflow-wrap: anywhere`), very long street names, and translated Marathi/Hindi text that is 30-40% longer[cite: 1]

